    "problem_statement": "### HTML Table Rendering Issue\n\n#### Description\nThere seems to be an issue with rendering HTML tables using the `PrettyTable` library. The HTML output does not match the expected format, leading to discrepancies in the rendered tables.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\nHere's a simple example to illustrate the problem:\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable\n\n# Create a PrettyTable instance\ntable = PrettyTable()\ntable.field_names = [\"Field 1\", \"Field 2\"]\ntable.add_row([\"Value 1\", \"Value 2\"])\ntable.add_row([\"Value 3\", \"Value 4\"])\n\n# Generate HTML output\nhtml_output = table.get_html_string()\nprint(html_output)\n```\n\n#### Expected Results\nThe HTML output should correctly format the table with appropriate tags and structure, ensuring that all data is displayed as expected.\n\n#### Actual Results\nThe HTML output is not formatted correctly, leading to issues in the display of the table. This affects the rendering of headers, data rows, and potentially the application of any specified attributes or styles.\n\n#### Additional Information\nThis issue seems to affect various aspects of HTML table generation, including the handling of headers and data escaping. It would be helpful to review the recent changes in the HTML rendering logic to identify any potential causes."
